WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.270
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:14.419
Today we are going to see an AI learn boxing
and even mimic gorillas during this process.

00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:21.770
Now, in an earlier work, we saw a few examples
of AI agents playing two-player sports, for

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:26.840
instance, this is the “You Shall Not Pass”
game, where the red agent is trying to hold

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.680
back the blue character and not let it cross
the line.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.710
Here you see two regular AIs duking it out,
sometimes the red wins, sometimes the blue

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.989
is able to get through.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:42.239
Nothing too crazy here.

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:45.519
Until…this happens.

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:47.159
Look.

00:00:47.159 --> 00:00:48.649
What is happening?

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:53.899
It seems that this agent started to do nothing…and
still won.

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:58.780
Not only that, but it suddenly started winning
almost all the games.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.739
How is this even possible?

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:06.270
Well, what the agent did is perhaps the AI
equivalent of hypnotizing the opponent, if

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.550
you will.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.760
The more rigorous term for this is that it
induces off-distribution activations in its

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:14.110
opponent.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:20.360
This adversarial agent is really doing nothing,
but that’s not enough - it is doing nothing

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:26.280
in a way that reprograms its opponent to make
mistakes and behave close to a completely

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.670
randomly acting agent!

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:34.810
Now, this new paper showcases AI agents that
can learn boxing.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:40.960
The AI is asked to control these joint-actuated
characters which are embedded in a physics

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:41.960
simulation.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:49.710
Well, that is quite a challenge - look, for
quite a while after 130 million steps of training,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.370
it cannot even hold it together.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.290
And, yes…these folks collapse.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:00.690
But this is not the good kind of hypnotic
adversarial collapsing.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:06.510
I am afraid, this is just passing out without
any particular benefits.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.950
That was quite a bit of training, and all
this for nearly nothing.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.020
Right?

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:17.959
Well, maybe…let’s see what they did after
200 million training steps.

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:18.959
Look!

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:24.530
They can not only hold it together, but they
have a little footwork going on, and can circle

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.760
each other and try to take the middle of the
ring.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.760
Improvements.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.760
Good.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.909
But this is not dancing practice, this is
boxing.

00:02:33.909 --> 00:02:39.300
I would really like to see some boxing today
and it doesn’t seem to happen.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:45.780
Until we wait for a little longer…which
is 250 million training steps.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.049
Now, is this boxing?

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:53.889
Not quite, this is more like two drunkards
trying to duke it out, where neither of them

00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:56.330
knows how to throw a real punch…but!

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:02.719
Their gloves are starting to touch the opponent,
and they start getting rewards for it.

00:03:02.719 --> 00:03:05.230
What does that mean for an intelligent agent?

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:11.340
Well, it means that over time, it will learn
to do that a little better.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:17.319
And hold on to your papers and see what they
do after 420 million steps.

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:19.209
Oh wow!

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:20.349
Look at that!

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:26.129
I am seeing some punches, and not only that,
but I also see some body and head movement

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:30.079
to evade the punches, very cool.

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:35.959
And if we keep going for longer, whoa!

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:37.689
These guys can fight!

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:45.530
They now learned to perform feints, jabs,
and have some proper knockout power too.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:50.680
And if you have been holding on to your papers,
now, squeeze that paper, because all they

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.700
looked at before starting the training was
90 seconds of motion capture data.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.220
This is a general framework that also works
for fencing as well.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.329
Look!

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:08.920
The agents learned to lunge, deflect, evade
attacks, and more.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.029
Absolutely amazing.

00:04:11.029 --> 00:04:12.650
What a time to be alive!

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.760
So, this was approximately a billion training
steps, right.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.419
So how long did that take to compute?

00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:23.620
It took approximately a week.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.090
And, you know what’s coming.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:31.910
Of course, we invoke the First Law Of Papers,
which says that research is a process.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:37.580
Do not look at where we are, look at where
we will be two more papers down the line.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:43.900
And two more papers down the line, I bet this
will be possible in a matter of hours.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.520
This is the part with the gorillas.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.000
It is also interesting that even though there
were plenty of reasons to, the researchers

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.440
didn’t quit after a 130 million steps.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:59.000
They just kept on going, and eventually, succeeded.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.810
Especially in the presence of not so trivial
training curves where the blocking of the

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:09.419
other player can worsen the performance, and
it’s often not as easy to tell where we

00:05:09.419 --> 00:05:10.509
are.

00:05:10.509 --> 00:06:03.120
That is a great life lesson right there.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.150
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

